WINDSHEAR



Windshear = the wind changes suddenly, in speed or direction, over a short distance.



Normal flight:         wind 20 kt ─────────────────── wind 20 kt   (calm, no problem)
Windshear:             headwind 30 kt ──┐
                                        └── tailwind 30 kt          (60 kt change in 1-2 NM!)



A plane flies on its airspeed (the air moving over the wings).

Headwind suddenly gets stronger → airspeed goes up → the plane climbs. That's OK.
Tailwind suddenly → airspeed goes down → the wings lose lift → the plane sinks. That's DANGEROUS.



High up, that's just a bump. Near the ground (takeoff and landing), the plane is slow and low, and there's no height to recover. That's why windshear crashes happen (Delta 191, Dallas 1985)



